= Introduction

== Overview

*Calisthenics* is an exacting and niche sport. Unlike regular hypertrophy training, Calisthenics requires "good form", which mandates a high level of kinesthetic awareness; and unlike other sports, Calisthenics is demanding on the central nervous system (CNS), which disallows frequent voluminous sessions. Calisthenics has not received adequate scientific attention. Most beginners rely on experienced athletes for advice, which, due to anatomical disparity, might not be appropriate for them. Although it is rising in popularity, the sport is still not an "official" one, so "official" guidelines are still non-existent. People start Calisthenics due to its affordability and accessibility, but advanced movements necessitate time-consuming personal exploration or expensive coaching.

Given these complications, a reliable spotter, a systematic program, and a detailed log are non-negotiable. All of these are frustrating burdens that sometimes deprive the training of its carefree enjoyment.

*Isometry* aims to reduce the friction in the fragmented administrative process by collapsing these drudgeries into a concentrated platform. Isometry's interface clearly specifies necessary training blocks and offers flexible grouping to allow users to have a holistic, manageable program. The platform also has an auto-logging feature, with the intra-set recording and data attached, to alleviate this burden from practitioner's mind. Isometry also integrates *Tracer*, an AI that empowers users to be their own spotter, giving them accompanying form analysis to locate imperfections and weak links.

== Program

The *Program Page* (or workspace) accord users total organizational agency. Exercises can be seamlessly grouped, customized, tagged, and fit into sessions with Isometry's navigationally intuitive and aesthetically pleasing interface. We designed the interface so that users start with listing desired exercise groups which are reflective of their current coveted skills. Into each of their created group, users can add specific exercises under pre-determined subcategories: straight/bent arm dynamic, and supported/unsupported static. Each group and exercise can be tagged and customized to taste. With these exercises drawn from their groups, users then proceed to design training sessions, which they finally add to their schedule. The schedule view will display basic analytics, such as rough estimation of the intra- and inter-session intensity, volume, and training frequency to help users adjust their training to their pace and recovery cycle.

== Log

Isometry has an auto-logging feature, whose sole input is the recording of a training set. Properties (such as hold time, rep number, form rectitude, posterior pelvic tilt, etc) and the set's clip are display in a set's log entry. Entries are created automatically based on user's training schedule. Users can customize their entries by changing what properties to display, by grouping log entries by group, by formatting entries' appearence, and adding descriptions.


== Spotter

Our app integrates Tracer, an AI that will analyze set recordings to output data for logging. Users can also feed the AI recordings from outside the app to access the analysis.